n {:value "2006127"} "Singles, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "2006203"} "Team, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "19004188"} "2020"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/2573"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Doubles, Mixed" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "27 – 30 July 2024"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2165"} "Paris Expo Porte de Versailles, Paris, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2006304"} "32 from 16 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "As in the two team events, the mixed doubles also consisted of sixteen NOCs, each with a pair of table tennis players. The initial quarter of the total quota was awarded to the semi-finalists of the designated qualifying meet, scheduled for March/April 2024, while six continental qualification tournaments (Africa, Asia, Europe, and Oceania, with the Americas, divided between North and Latin, for an ITTF competition) offered a mixed doubles spot each to the top-ranked NOC from their respective continent. Apart from the host nation, the five highest-ranked eligible pairs obtained the remaining berths based on the ITTF World Ranking list of May 7, 2024."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Chinese duo, " [:a {:href "/athletes/2502769"} "Wang Chuqin"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/2504735"} "Sun Yingsha"] ", entered the competition as the very clear favorites. They were not only World No.1 in doubles, but also led the world single’s ranking going into the Olympics. As a pair who were undefeated outside China for over four years, and boasting four WTT Series titles in 2024 alone, they were on a mission to reclaim the gold that slipped through Chinese fingers in Tokyo. Their track record was nothing short of jaw-dropping, with only six losses in their senior career together since 2018."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The duo easily reached the final, where they surprisingly met the unseeded duo from North Korea, " [:a {:href "/athletes/155162"} "Ri Jong-Sik"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/155161"} "Kim Kum-Yong"] ". After a thrilling match, the Chinese pair won by 4-2, claiming China’s first table tennis gold in Paris. This first one was followed by another four, resulting for the first time at the Olympics China having a clean sweep of all events, winning five gold medals. Unfortunately, it wasn’t a clean sweep for Wang and Sun, as she lost the women’s final and he was surprisingly eliminated in the first round of the men’s singles."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/2502769"} "Wang Chuqin"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/2504735"} "Sun Yingsha"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/155162"} "Ri Jong-Sik"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/155161"} "Kim Kum-Yong"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/PRK"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/PRK.png"}] "PRK"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/155150"} "Im Jong-Hun"] " / " [:a {:hre